CAT = "ea2Records"; SEG = "REC"
# Financial records, accounting methods, related parties and advising the business taxpayer.
Q = [
("Schedule M-1 of Form 1120 reconciles:",
 "Book income with taxable income", ["Retained earnings from year to year", "Beginning and ending assets", "Cash income with accrual income"],
 "Schedule M-1 starts from net income per books and lists the items that differ for tax to reach taxable income. Schedule M-2 analyzes retained earnings, and Schedule L is the balance sheet."),
("On Schedule M-1, federal income tax expense recorded on the books is:",
 "Added back to book income", ["Subtracted from book income", "Left out of the schedule", "Shown only on Schedule M-2"],
 "Federal income tax is not deductible, so the book expense is added back to book income to move toward taxable income."),
("On Schedule M-1, tax-exempt interest included in book income is:",
 "Subtracted from book income", ["Added to book income", "Left in taxable income", "Reported as other income"],
 "Tax-exempt interest is income on the books but not taxable, so it appears among the items recorded on the books but not included on the return and is subtracted."),
("A corporation's ending retained earnings on Schedule L should agree with:",
 "The ending balance on Schedule M-2", ["The last line of Schedule M-1", "Taxable income on page 1", "Beginning cash on Schedule L"],
 "Schedule M-2 analyzes unappropriated retained earnings per books from the beginning to the end of the year, so its ending balance should match retained earnings on the year-end balance sheet."),
("Under the accrual method, an expense is deductible once all events fix the liability, the amount is reasonably determinable, and:",
 "Economic performance has occurred", ["The bill has been paid in full", "The vendor's invoice is received", "The tax year has closed"],
 "Section 461(h) adds the economic performance requirement: generally, services or property must be provided, or for some liabilities payment made, before the deduction is allowed."),
("A C corporation may use the cash method for 2025 if it:",
 "Meets the gross receipts test", ["Has fewer than 100 employees", "Is owned by five or fewer people", "Holds no inventory at all"],
 "Section 448 bars C corporations from the cash method unless they meet the section 448(c) average gross receipts test or are qualified personal service corporations or farming businesses."),
("A business switching from the cash method to the accrual method generally needs to file:",
 "Form 3115", ["Form 1128", "Form 8832", "Form 7004"],
 "A change in overall accounting method needs IRS consent, requested on Form 3115, often under the automatic procedures. Form 1128 is for changing the tax year."),
("A method change produces a positive section 481(a) adjustment. It is generally taken into income over:",
 "4 tax years", ["1 tax year", "2 tax years", "10 tax years"],
 "The Form 3115 instructions spread a positive adjustment over the year of change and the next 3 years. A negative adjustment is generally taken all in the year of change."),
("A business that uses a hybrid method commonly uses:",
 "Accrual for purchases and sales", ["Cash for purchases and sales", "Accrual only for its expenses", "Cash only for its inventory"],
 "A business that must account for inventories may use the accrual method for purchases and sales and the cash method for other items, as long as the combination clearly reflects income."),
("A partner's share of the partnership's nondeductible expenses:",
 "Reduces outside basis", ["Increases outside basis", "Has no basis effect", "Is deducted on Schedule E"],
 "Section 705(a)(2)(B) decreases a partner's basis by the share of expenditures that are not deductible and not capital, such as the disallowed half of meals and penalties."),
("For the section 267 loss rules, which person is not a member of Ed's family?",
 "His sister-in-law", ["His half brother", "His grandmother", "His daughter"],
 "Section 267(c)(4) counts only brothers and sisters (whole or half blood), a spouse, ancestors and lineal descendants as family. In-laws are not included, so a loss sale to a sister-in-law is not disallowed by that rule."),
("A corporation lends its sole shareholder $200,000 interest-free, payable on demand. The result is:",
 "Imputed interest and a dividend", ["Imputed interest and wages", "No tax effect for either", "Interest income to the owner"],
 "Section 7872 treats a below-market corporation-shareholder loan as if the corporation paid the forgone interest to the shareholder as a dividend and the shareholder paid it back as interest. Wages arise only in an employer-employee loan."),
("A corporation advances cash to its shareholder with no note, no interest and no repayment schedule. The advances are likely:",
 "Treated as dividends", ["Treated as loans", "Treated as wages", "Ignored until repaid"],
 "Without the marks of a real loan, such as a note, interest, a fixed schedule and actual repayment, the IRS may treat withdrawals as constructive dividends to the extent of earnings and profits."),
("A car dealer receives $6,000 in currency for a car and, three weeks later, $5,000 more in currency from the same buyer for that car. The dealer:",
 "Must file Form 8300", ["Need not file Form 8300", "Files only if one payment tops $10,000", "Files Form 1099-MISC instead"],
 "Related payments for one transaction are added together for Form 8300, so once the total exceeds $10,000 the dealer must file within 15 days of the payment that pushes it over."),
("A business generally need not issue Form 1099-NEC for $3,000 of services paid to:",
 "A C corporation", ["A sole proprietor", "A partnership", "An LLC taxed as a partnership"],
 "Payments to corporations are generally exempt from Form 1099-NEC reporting. Payments to attorneys are an exception and are reported even when the law firm is a corporation."),
("A contractor refuses to give a business a taxpayer identification number. The business must backup withhold at:",
 "24%", ["28%", "22%", "30%"],
 "Section 3406 sets backup withholding at the fourth-lowest individual rate, 24% since 2018. The withholding is reported on Form 945."),
("An employer that reported $45,000 of Form 941 taxes in its lookback period deposits employment taxes:",
 "Monthly", ["Semiweekly", "Quarterly", "Annually"],
 "Publication 15 makes an employer a monthly schedule depositor for the year if lookback-period taxes were $50,000 or less, and a semiweekly depositor above that."),
("A monthly depositor accumulates $105,000 of employment taxes on one day. The taxes must be deposited by:",
 "The next business day", ["The 15th of next month", "The next Friday", "The end of the quarter"],
 "Under the $100,000 next-day deposit rule, any employer that accumulates $100,000 or more of tax on any day must deposit by the next business day, and becomes a semiweekly depositor."),
("Under an accountable plan, expense reimbursements to employees are:",
 "Excluded from their wages", ["Included in their wages", "Reported on Form 1099-NEC", "Taxed when over $75"],
 "Reimbursements under an accountable plan are not wages and are not reported on Form W-2, as long as the employee substantiates expenses and returns any excess within a reasonable period."),
("To keep an allowance arrangement accountable, employees must substantiate expenses and also:",
 "Return any excess paid", ["Sign a new Form W-4", "Charge a company card", "Submit receipts weekly"],
 "Publication 15 requires a business connection, substantiation within a reasonable period and return of amounts above substantiated expenses. Excess an employee keeps is wages."),
("Adequate records for business use of a car show, for each trip, the miles, the date, the destination and:",
 "The business purpose", ["The fuel cost", "The passengers' names", "An odometer photo"],
 "Section 274(d) and Publication 463 require records of the amount, time, place and business purpose of each use. Fuel receipts support actual expenses but do not prove business use."),
("Under common-law rules, a worker is most likely an employee when the business has the right to:",
 "Control how the work is done", ["Pay for the work hourly", "Report the pay on Form 1099", "Label the job as freelance"],
 "The common-law test looks at behavioral control, financial control and the relationship. The right to direct and control how the work is done is the key mark of employment, whatever a contract calls the worker."),
("Section 530 relief can protect a business that treated workers as contractors if it filed all required Forms 1099, had a reasonable basis, and:",
 "Treated similar workers alike", ["Paid each over $600 a year", "Withheld income tax for them", "Signed contracts with each"],
 "Section 530 of the Revenue Act of 1978 requires reporting consistency, substantive consistency (no similar worker treated as an employee) and a reasonable basis. Withholding would show employee treatment."),
("For 2025 the employer shared responsibility rules apply to an employer that last year averaged at least:",
 "50 full-time and equivalent employees", ["25 full-time and equivalent employees", "75 full-time and equivalent employees", "100 full-time and equivalent employees"],
 "Section 4980H applies to an applicable large employer, one with an average of at least 50 full-time employees, counting full-time equivalents, in the prior calendar year."),
("Under the Affordable Care Act employer rules, a full-time employee averages at least:",
 "30 hours a week", ["40 hours a week", "35 hours a week", "20 hours a week"],
 "Section 4980H(c)(4) defines a full-time employee as one employed an average of at least 30 hours of service a week, or 130 hours a month."),
("A main tax drawback of a C corporation compared with an S corporation is that:",
 "Its profits can be taxed twice", ["Its owners lack limited liability", "It may have only 100 owners", "Its profits bear SE tax"],
 "A C corporation pays tax on its income and shareholders pay tax again on dividends. An S corporation's income passes through once; the 100-shareholder limit applies to S corporations, not C corporations."),
("A shareholder pays personal bills from the corporate account without recording them. The main tax risk is:",
 "Constructive dividends", ["Section 179 recapture", "Loss of the cash method", "Automatic S termination"],
 "Corporate payments of an owner's personal expenses are not business deductions and may be treated as constructive dividends, or wages if paid as compensation, to the owner."),
("The trust fund recovery penalty for unpaid withheld employment taxes may be assessed against:",
 "Willful responsible persons", ["Only the business entity", "Any employee who signs checks", "Only an outside payroll firm"],
 "Section 6672 makes any person responsible for collecting and paying over trust fund taxes who willfully fails to do so personally liable for 100% of the unpaid trust fund amount."),
]
